menu "pairble: Wi-Fi provisioning"

    config PAIRBLE_SOFTAP
        bool "Also offer setup over the device's own Wi-Fi (SoftAP)"
        default y
        help
            Runs a WPA2 access point alongside BLE while setup is open, named
            after the device, with the proof of possession as its password.
            For phones that cannot use Bluetooth: an iPhone with no internet
            to download the App Clip, a browser without Web Bluetooth, a
            laptop. Costs RAM and shares the one 2.4 GHz radio with BLE.

    config PAIRBLE_CAPTIVE_PORTAL
        bool "Pop the setup page up automatically (captive portal)"
        default y
        depends on PAIRBLE_SOFTAP
        help
            Answers every DNS lookup on the access point with its own address
            and redirects every unknown URL to the setup page, which makes most
            phones open it on their own after joining. A convenience only: it
            does not work behind custom DNS or a VPN, and iOS 26 has been
            reported not to show the sheet, so always print "open
            http://192.168.4.1" on the label too.

    config PAIRBLE_SERVICE_UUID
        string "BLE service UUID"
        default "021a9004-0382-4aea-bff4-6b3f1c5adfb4"
        help
            The primary GATT service advertised while provisioning. The default
            is the one used by the ESP-IDF provisioning example, which the
            pairble clients also default to. Set your own for a product so
            that the browser's chooser does not offer somebody else's device.

    config PAIRBLE_NAME_PREFIX
        string "Device name prefix"
        default "PAIRBLE-"
        help
            Unless the application sets a name, the device advertises
            "<prefix><last three bytes of the MAC>", e.g. PAIRBLE-BA6550.
            Upper case and a hyphen by default because the name is typed by
            hand when a QR code will not scan: no doubt about letter case, and
            a hyphen sits on a phone's first keyboard layer, an underscore
            does not.
            At most 23 characters. Print the full name on the box.

    config PAIRBLE_WIFI_CONN_ATTEMPTS
        int "Connection attempts before reporting failure"
        default 2
        range 1 10
        help
            How often the device retries the credentials it was given before
            telling the phone they failed. Measured against real hardware,
            five attempts made a wrong password take 54 seconds to report,
            the first 15 of them with nothing to show the user. Another retry
            does not fix a wrong password, so keep this low.

    config PAIRBLE_POP_GENERATE
        bool "Generate a proof of possession on first boot if none is stored"
        default y
        help
            With no PoP passed in and none in NVS, generate a random one on
            first boot and keep it in NVS. Right for devices that show their
            QR code on a display or in the log. Turn it off for products with
            printed labels, where the PoP must come from @pairble/cli so that
            it matches the label, and a missing one is a production fault.

    config PAIRBLE_LOG_SETUP_URL
        bool "Log the setup URL and the Wi-Fi QR code"
        default y if PAIRBLE_POP_GENERATE
        default n
        help
            Both contain the proof of possession, which lets anyone in radio
            range set the device up and join its SoftAP. Handy on a
            development board, or for a device that generates its own PoP and
            has nowhere else to show it; off by default for products whose
            PoP comes printed on a label, because serial logs end up in
            support tickets and CI output. The device name is logged either
            way.

    config PAIRBLE_SETUP_URL
        string "Setup page URL encoded in the QR code"
        default "https://pairble.com/s"
        help
            The device name and PoP are appended in the URL fragment
            (#name=...&pop=...), which never reaches the server.

            pairble.com is free to use, in production too, with no limits:
            the setup page and the pairble App Clip are registered there.

            Your own domain works as well, but an iPhone then needs an App
            Clip of your own, approved by Apple and associated with that
            domain - ideally as part of your own app. Write to
            support@pairble.com to set that up.

    config PAIRBLE_BLE_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS
        int "Drop a BLE client that idles this long (0 = never)"
        default 300
        range 0 3600
        depends on BT_NIMBLE_ENABLED
        help
            While a phone is connected the device stops advertising, so no
            other phone can find it. Measured on hardware: a browser tab left
            open on a failure screen kept a device invisible for 22 minutes.
            The pairble clients let go on their own after two idle minutes;
            this is the backstop for clients that do not.

            The clock runs from the moment a phone connects, stops while the
            device is trying credentials it received, and starts again after a
            failed attempt, when the person may have walked away mid-retry.
            Generous on purpose: the clients reconnect transparently, but a
            person looking up their password should not be cut off.

            NimBLE only; with Bluedroid there is no guard.

    config PAIRBLE_WINDOW_SECONDS
        int "Provisioning window in seconds (0 = until provisioned)"
        default 0
        help
            How long first-time setup stays open on a device with no stored
            network. Reopened setup (see below) has its own window.

    menu "When the stored network stops working"

        config PAIRBLE_RECOVERY_AFTER_SECONDS
            int "Reopen setup after this long offline (0 = never)"
            default 120
            range 0 86400
            help
                A device that has been offline this long, counted across
                deep sleep, opens setup again on its own - the way back for
                someone who replaced their router or changed its password.

                Nothing is erased. The stored network is retried while setup
                is open, and setup closes as soon as it answers, so a router
                that was merely restarting costs nothing. New credentials
                replace the old ones only once the device has joined with
                them.

                Two minutes is longer than a typical router takes to boot.

        config PAIRBLE_RECOVERY_WINDOW_SECONDS
            int "Keep reopened setup open for (seconds)"
            default 600
            range 60 86400
            help
                After this, setup closes, the device goes back to retrying
                the stored network, and setup reopens after another
                PAIRBLE_RECOVERY_AFTER_SECONDS offline.

        config PAIRBLE_RECOVERY_RETRY_SECONDS
            int "While setup is open, retry the stored network every (seconds)"
            default 30
            range 10 600
            help
                Paused while a phone is connected, because the device has
                one radio: a connection attempt would make the phone's
                network scan fail.

        config PAIRBLE_SETUP_BUTTON_GPIO
            int "Button that reopens setup (GPIO, -1 = none)"
            default -1
            range -1 48
            help
                Holding this button reopens setup, keeping the stored
                network. pairble configures the pin as an input with the
                internal pull resistor. On most development boards the BOOT
                button is GPIO 0 (GPIO 9 on ESP32-C3/C6).

        config PAIRBLE_SETUP_BUTTON_ACTIVE_LOW
            bool "The button pulls the pin low"
            default y
            depends on PAIRBLE_SETUP_BUTTON_GPIO >= 0

        config PAIRBLE_SETUP_BUTTON_HOLD_SECONDS
            int "Hold the button for (seconds)"
            default 5
            range 1 30
            depends on PAIRBLE_SETUP_BUTTON_GPIO >= 0

        config PAIRBLE_POWER_CYCLE_COUNT
            int "Reopen setup after this many quick power cycles (0 = never)"
            default 3
            range 0 10
            help
                For devices with no button - a bulb, a plug: switching it on
                this many times in a row, each time for less than
                PAIRBLE_POWER_CYCLE_SECONDS, reopens setup. Power-on and
                brownout resets count (so does pulling EN, which reports as
                a power-on), not crashes or esp_restart(). Nothing is erased,
                so an accidental trigger is harmless.

        config PAIRBLE_POWER_CYCLE_SECONDS
            int "A power cycle is quick if the device was on for less than (seconds)"
            default 5
            range 2 60
            depends on PAIRBLE_POWER_CYCLE_COUNT > 0

        config PAIRBLE_FAST_RECONNECT
            bool "Remember the access point and channel for faster reconnects"
            default n
            help
                Skips the full channel scan on the next connection by
                aiming at the access point and channel that worked last
                time; if that fails once, pairble forgets them and scans.
                Worth it on battery devices that wake to send one reading.

    endmenu

    choice PAIRBLE_BT_MEMORY
        prompt "Bluetooth memory after setup"
        default PAIRBLE_BT_MEMORY_RELEASE_CLASSIC
        help
            Released memory can never be used by Bluetooth again until the
            next reset, which is why the default keeps BLE's.

        config PAIRBLE_BT_MEMORY_KEEP
            bool "Keep all (the application uses Bluetooth too)"
            help
                For applications that use Bluetooth Classic or BLE
                themselves once setup is over.

        config PAIRBLE_BT_MEMORY_RELEASE_CLASSIC
            bool "Release Bluetooth Classic, keep BLE"
            help
                pairble uses only BLE. Setup can reopen without a restart.
                Chips without Bluetooth Classic have nothing to release.

        config PAIRBLE_BT_MEMORY_RELEASE_ALL
            bool "Release all of it"
            help
                The most free RAM once setup is over, but reopening setup
                then restarts the device, because BLE cannot come back
                without a reset.
    endchoice

    config PAIRBLE_IMPROV_UART
        bool "Improv Wi-Fi over serial on a UART"
        default n
        help
            Speaks Improv Wi-Fi's serial protocol on a UART, so the ESP Web
            Tools installer (flash in the browser, then set Wi-Fi),
            improv-wifi.com and Home Assistant can set the network over USB.
            The network is treated like one from a phone: the stored one
            stays until the new one works. Log output on the same UART is
            fine. For USB Serial/JTAG consoles, or Arduino, feed the port to
            pairble_improv_input() yourself (see pairble.h).

            Whoever has the USB cable can change the network: the serial port
            has no security of its own, by Improv's design.

    config PAIRBLE_IMPROV_UART_NUM
        int "UART"
        default 0
        range 0 2
        depends on PAIRBLE_IMPROV_UART

    config PAIRBLE_IMPROV_URL
        string "Address to offer once online ({ip} = the device's)"
        default ""
        depends on PAIRBLE_IMPROV_UART
        help
            For example "http://{ip}/" for a device with its own web page.
            Empty: none.

    config PAIRBLE_REBOOT_AFTER_SUCCESS
        bool "Reboot after successful provisioning"
        default y
        help
            Frees the Bluetooth stack's memory for good and starts the
            application from a clean state with the stored credentials.

endmenu
